# Gallstones

- Gallstones (Cholelithiasis) - stone in the gallbladder
- **Causal factors in gallstone formation:**
  - **3 types of gallstones** - cholesterol, pigment (brown/black), mixed
  - **Cholesterol stone** - solubility of cholesterol dependent on balance between concentration of bile acids and cholesterol:
- **Clinical presentation of gallstones:**
  - Asymptomatic - detected incidentally
  - Biliary colic - misnomer because not often colicky, but dull and persistent:
    - Site - RUQ or epigastric pain
    - Onset - sudden and lasts for minutes to several hours (due to impaction of stones on cystic duct)
    - Character - colicky or dull and persistent
    - Radiation - to the back
    - Associated Sx - N/V, dyspeptic Sx
    - Time course - repeated episodes, persistent to up to a few hours
    - Exacerbating factors - fatty foods
  - Persistence of Sx with fever - suggestive of a diagnosis of acute cholecystitis
- **Complications:**
  - Cholecystitis and cholecystitis related complications - mucocele of gallbladder, empyema of the gallbladder, perforation of the gallbladder
  - Complications related to migration of stone:
    - Choledocholithiasis (stone obstructing CBD)
    - Acute cholangitis
    - Acute pancreatitis
    - Gallstone ileus
- **Dx** - on compatible Hx, P/E and transabdominal ultrasound
  - Transamdonal US - high sensitivity and specificity:
    - Findings - acoustic shadowing (US artifact) caused by gallstones

    
    ![](img/gallstone_us.png)
- **Mx:**
  - No Mx required for asymptomatic patients
  - Cholecystectomy required if symptomatic or complications developed
  - **Conservative Mx for cholecystitis**:
    - Nil per mouth (NPO) and IV fluid administration until pain resolves
    - Administration of analgesics
    - IV ABx - conc. of ABx in serum more important than that in bile (as cystic duct is blocked)
    - Cholecystectomy - performed on the next available list

